\section{Preserve Direction During Inference}

An equality constraint identifies types; a subtype constraint puts them in order without identifying them. That difference is why replacing every equation of Hindley--Milner inference with a subtype test is not an algorithm. In particular, unifying two type variables merges everything known about them, whereas an edge $\alpha \leq \beta$ allows a proper subtype at one end. Martin Odersky, Martin Sulzmann, and Martin Wehr's \emph{Type Inference with Constrained Types} separates inference from the choice of constraint theory in the framework $\mathrm{HM}(X)$. Sound inference and principal-type results depend on properties of that theory. You-Chin Fuh and Prateek Mishra's \emph{Type Inference with Subtypes} also studies principal typings and matching in a setting with an explicit subtype theory; these are not results about arbitrary combinations of subtyping features.

In a small structural solver, a use of a value whose type is $S$ in a position expecting $T$ emits $S\leq T$. Arrow decomposition reverses the direction for domains and preserves it for results:
\[
   (S_1\to S_2)\leq(T_1\to T_2)
   \quad\Longrightarrow\quad T_1\leq S_1,\qquad S_2\leq T_2.
\]
The implementation can keep lower and upper bound sets for each metavariable. When a new lower bound $L$ is attached to a variable already bounded above by $U$, enqueue $L\leq U$. Propagate along variable-to-variable edges and record visited obligations to avoid processing the same edge repeatedly. A failed comparison between concrete types is a contradiction. Unification is still useful for actual equality obligations, but ordinary directed edges must not become union-find merges.

\section{Worked Bounds: A Consumer and Its Arguments}

Take a nominal hierarchy with $\mathsf{Dog},\mathsf{Cat}\leq \mathsf{Animal}\leq\mathsf{Object}$ and no other common supertypes below $\mathsf{Object}$ except $\mathsf{Animal}$. A combinator has the type scheme
\[
  \mathsf{consumeBoth} :
  \forall a.\ (a\to\mathsf{Unit})\to a\to a\to\mathsf{Unit}.
\]
It calls its callback on each of the two values. Instantiate $a$ with a fresh $\alpha$. Applying the combinator to a callback of type $\mathsf{Animal}\to\mathsf{Unit}$, then to a dog and a cat, generates
\[
  \alpha \leq \mathsf{Animal},\qquad
  \mathsf{Dog} \leq \alpha,\qquad \mathsf{Cat} \leq \alpha.
\]
The first inequality comes from the callback argument, where domain contravariance is essential. In this hierarchy the lower bounds have join $\mathsf{Animal}$, which also satisfies the upper bound, so choosing $\alpha=\mathsf{Animal}$ yields a concrete core application. With a dog-only callback the new upper bound would be $\mathsf{Dog}$ and propagation would require $\mathsf{Cat}\leq\mathsf{Dog}$. A diagnostic can point to the cat argument and the callback signature rather than rejecting the last syntactic application without explanation.

If the callback accepted $\mathsf{Object}$, the bounds would also admit $\mathsf{Object}$, even though $\mathsf{Animal}$ is the least solution in this example. A join may not exist in a system with richer nominal inheritance, and selecting a representative by declaration order would make inference unstable. For a local type argument, an annotation or a specified selection policy can resolve this situation; for a generalized binding, a constrained scheme can preserve the range of solutions.

\section{Generalize Constraints, Not Guesses}

A schema such as $\forall\alpha.\ (L\leq\alpha \wedge \alpha\leq U)\Rightarrow T$ says what must hold each time $T$ is instantiated. At a generalization point, solve or simplify what the local theory permits; quantify only variables not fixed by the surrounding environment, subject also to restrictions on mutable state (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-144}). At use sites, instantiate the quantified variables freshly and copy their residual constraints. An implementation that wishes to hide a private variable from an exported signature must actually eliminate it, for example by projection in a theory that admits projection. Merely deleting the obligations in which it occurs can weaken the signature unsoundly.

One check on simplification is whether it preserves the set of assignments to the visible variables that can be extended to solutions. For example, eliminating an otherwise unconstrained private $\alpha$ between $L$ and $U$ leaves $L\leq U$: necessity follows from transitivity and sufficiency by choosing $\alpha=L$. This argument need not apply if $\alpha$ also occurs under a type constructor or only some kinds of types are admissible as solutions.

Test both satisfiability and the chosen elaborations. Two derivations of a subtype relation may induce observably different coercions when the language admits user-defined conversions, requiring a coherence rule or explicit disambiguation. Even if coercions erase, restricting the constraint language may be necessary to keep solving tractable and messages readable. Markdown Dossier Chapter~37 discusses broader compiler uses of constraint solving; type inference additionally needs a policy for unknowns that survive a successful solve.
